<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Tasmania</h1><p class="st-meta">1825 CE – 1901 CE · capital Hobart</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Australia's island colony, notorious for its convict stations and the near-destruction of its Aboriginal people, before it joined the federation in 1901.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> settled Van Diemen's Land in 1803 to forestall the French, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a> was founded in 1804. Administered at first from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-new-south-wales">Colony of New South Wales</a>, it became a separate colony in 1825, under Lieutenant Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-arthur">George Arthur</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The island received around 75,000 convicts, held at stations such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-arthur-tasmania">Port Arthur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macquarie-harbour">Macquarie Harbour</a>, and became a disciplined penal society that also exported wool and whale oil. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-war">Black War</a> of the 1820s colonists and soldiers killed many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a>, and the survivors were removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wybalenna">Wybalenna</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flinders-island">Flinders Island</a> in the 1830s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penal-transportation">Transportation</a> ended in 1853, and in 1856 the colony won self-government and adopted the name Tasmania.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tasmania stayed small, with mining at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-bischoff">Mount Bischoff</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lyell">Mount Lyell</a> later in the century. It voted for federation and became a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia</a> in 1901. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-inglis-clark">Andrew Inglis Clark</a>, its attorney general, was a key drafter of the Australian Constitution.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-tasmania.jpg" alt="Colony of Tasmania" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author (Proclaimed by Sir Frederick Weld) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tasmania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1825 CE – 1901 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hobart</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>George Arthur, John Franklin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Van Diemen's Land, Tasmania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1863&amp;focus=colony-of-tasmania" aria-label="Colony of Tasmania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8239 3701 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Tasmania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8559 3957L8856 3661M8559 3957L4997 651M8559 3957L8774 3709"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8856 3661h0M4997 651h0M8774 3709h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8559 3957h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8559 3957h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hobart. <a href="/atlas?year=1863&amp;focus=colony-of-tasmania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
